  "summary": "Distinctive architectural style of the Maya civilization characterized by intricate stone mosaics and ornate facades.",
  "description": "Features detailed geometric patterns and deity masks carved on limestone facades, reflecting the Maya's sophisticated stone-working techniques and religious iconography. Developed during the Terminal Classic period as regional centers like Chichén Itzá flourished.",
  "culturalContext": "Chichén Itzá fused Puuc hills masonry with Toltec-influenced forms in the Terminal Classic era (roughly 800-1100 CE), when the city dominated Yucatán trade and pilgrimage. Elite buildings — El Castillo pyramid-temple, the Warriors' temple with its Chacmool altar, the largest ballcourt in Mesoamerica — carried Maya calendar symbolism and feathered-serpent cults at imperial scale; the site anchored Maya-Toltec interaction and remains Mexico's most visited monument.",
